    "stdout": "RISC-V emulator for ESP32-C3/C6/P4 IDF binaries\n\nUsage: esp-emu [OPTIONS] --chip <CHIP> --firmware <FIRMWARE>\n\nOptions:\n      --chip <CHIP>\n          Target chip\n          \n          [possible values: esp32c3, esp32c5, esp32c6, esp32h2, esp32p4,\n          esp32s3, esp32s31]\n\n      --firmware <FIRMWARE>\n          Path to firmware binary (merged flash image)\n          \n          Download mode (--strap-mode 0x02/0x04) never boots firmware, so only\n          there may this flag be omitted; the ROM loader then sees a blank flash\n          (esptool/espefuse still work).\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n\n  -V, --version\n          Print version\n\nNetworking:\n      --net <NET>\n          Network backend: \"user\", \"tap,ifname=tap0\", \"vmnet\"\n          \n          \"user\" is slirp-style with no host setup. When omitted, Linux tries\n          tap0 first and falls back to user; other OSes default to user.\n\n      --wifi-ssid <WIFI_SSID>\n          WiFi soft AP SSID (default: myssid)\n\n      --wifi-password <WIFI_PASSWORD>\n          WiFi passphrase (default: mypassword)\n          \n          Must be 8-63 chars. Pass \"\" for an open (unencrypted) AP.\n\n      --wifi-fw-ap-password <PASS>\n          Password of the firmware's own soft AP, used when the emulator joins\n          it (default: --wifi-password). Pass \"\" for an open AP\n\n      --wifi-auth <MODE>\n          Soft AP authentication: wpa2-wpa3, wpa3-sae, wpa2-psk, open\n          \n          Default is wpa2-wpa3 (transition: PSK and SAE offered, PMF capable),\n          which lets WPA2 and WPA3 firmware each negotiate what it supports; \"\"\n          as --wifi-password means open. Pin wpa3-sae (PMF required) or wpa2-psk\n          to test a firmware's security policy against that AP.\n\n      --wifi-radius <HOST:PORT,SECRET>\n          RADIUS server for the enterprise modes: HOST:PORT,SECRET\n          \n          EAP from the station is relayed to it (hostapd's EAP server or\n          FreeRADIUS); the MSK it returns becomes the PMK.\n\n      --wifi-sae-pwe <PWE>\n          SAE password element: both, h2e, hunt-and-peck (default: both)\n          \n          both and h2e advertise hash-to-element (RSNXE); hunt-and-peck hides it\n          so a station falls back to the looping derivation.\n\nBLE:\n      --elf <PATH>...\n          Application ELF(s) for BLE symbol interception (e.g.,\n          build/project.elf). Takes several paths, or may be repeated: at app\n          start the ELF whose SHA-256 the running app's descriptor records is\n          used, so after an OTA the new build's ELF takes over\n\n      --ble-hci <BLE_HCI>\n          HCI backend: \"tcp:host:port\" for Bumble, \"hci0\" for Linux adapter\n\nThread:\n      --thread-sim <THREAD_SIM>\n          802.15.4 bridge spec, e.g. \"bind:9001,peer:127.0.0.1:9002\"\n          \n          Forwards frames between the emulated IEEE 802.15.4 driver and a UDP\n          peer (another emulator, or OpenThread POSIX simulation).\n\nESP-Hosted:\n      --hosted <HOSTED>\n          ESP-Hosted SDIO bridge: bridge:host:/sock or bridge:slave:/sock\n          \n          `bridge:host:/path/to/sock` \u2014 P4 host instance, connects to slave.\n          `bridge:slave:/path/to/sock` \u2014 C6 slave instance, listens for host.\n          Start the slave first, then the host. Lossless Unix stream.\n\n      --hosted-reset-gpio <PIN>\n          P4 GPIO wired to the C6 EN/CHIP_PU reset pin (host side only).\n          \n          When the host firmware pulses this GPIO (the ESP-Hosted slave-reset\n          sequence), a RESET is forwarded over the bridge and the slave instance\n          reboots \u2014 modelling the reset wire, which is not part of the SDIO bus.\n          Must match the firmware's reset-GPIO Kconfig.\n\n      --hosted-reset-active-high\n          Treat the slave-reset GPIO as active-high (default: active-low / EN)\n\nROM:\n      --rom <ROM>\n          Path to ROM ELF file (e.g., esp32c3_rev3_rom.elf)\n          \n          When omitted, a default ROM embedded at build time is used for the\n          selected --chip.\n\nAdvanced:\n      --timeout <TIMEOUT>\n          Exit after this duration (e.g., \"5s\", \"10s\")\n\n      --exit-on <EXIT_ON>\n          Exit with code 0 when UART output contains this string\n\n      --save-state\n          Save flash/NVS state to disk on exit (overwrites firmware file)\n\n      --efuse <EFUSE>\n          Path to eFuse binary file (QEMU-compatible, 336 bytes)\n\n      --skip-bootloader\n          Skip bootloader: load app directly from partition table\n\n      --skip-rom\n          Skip the mask ROM; reset the CPU directly at the bootloader entry\n          \n          Default executes the real ROM, which is required for\n          KEY_SOURCE_KEY_MGR flash recovery and other eFuse-driven cold-boot\n          decisions.\n\n      --batch-size <BATCH_SIZE>\n          Batch size, instructions per iteration (default: 50000)\n\n      --inject <INJECT>\n          Payload to inject into UART RX (paired with --inject-on)\n          \n          Supports \\n / \\r escapes. Repeatable: each --inject is paired\n          positionally with the matching --inject-on trigger and fires once.\n\n      --inject-on <INJECT_ON>\n          Trigger string in UART TX output that fires the paired --inject\n          \n          Repeatable; each --inject-on is paired positionally with --inject.\n\n      --uart-tcp <HOST:PORT>\n          Bridge UART0 to a TCP server (e.g. \"127.0.0.1:5555\")\n          \n          Mirrors QEMU's `-serial tcp::PORT,server,nowait`. esptool can connect\n          via `socket://127.0.0.1:5555`. While active, UART0 RX comes from the\n          socket (not stdin) and UART0 TX goes to the socket (not stdout). A\n          USB-Serial/JTAG console (see --console) still prints to stdout. With\n          --strap-mode 0x04 the socket is the USB-Serial/JTAG download port.\n\n      --uart1-tcp <HOST:PORT>\n          Bridge UART1 to a TCP server (e.g. \"127.0.0.1:5556\")\n          \n          Side channel for simulating an external serial device (sensor, GPS,\n          modem) attached to UART1. Independent of --uart-tcp: stdin/stdout and\n          --exit-on / --inject-on keep tracking UART0. Without a connected\n          client, UART1 TX is discarded (like an unattached chardev in QEMU).\n\n      --control-tcp <HOST:PORT>\n          Serve a host control channel on this address (e.g. \"127.0.0.1:5556\")\n          \n          Newline-delimited commands, one reply per line:\n          \n          - `reset` \u2014 chip reset, what a DTR/RTS toggle does on hardware, which\n          a UART socket cannot express because pyserial's `socket://` ignores\n          modem control lines. `reset --soft` gives a software reset instead. -\n          `efuse-load <path>` \u2014 read a QEMU-compatible eFuse image into the\n          running machine and reset it, so bits burned by a download-mode\n          instance take effect without restarting the process. - `erase-region\n          <offset> <length>` and `erase-flash` \u2014 what `esptool erase-region` /\n          `erase-flash` do, applied to the running machine's flash so the\n          firmware sees it without a reload. - `write-region <offset> <file>` \u2014\n          as `esptool write-flash`, reading the data from a path because the\n          channel is line based. - `ping` \u2014 answers `ok`.\n\n      --gdb <PORT>\n          Serve a GDB remote stub on this TCP port (QEMU's `-s` is port 1234)\n          \n          riscv32-esp-elf-gdb connects with `target remote :PORT`. Supports\n          register/memory read+write, software breakpoints, single-step, and\n          continue \u2014 enough for scripted fault injection (breakpoint + `set\n          var=...` + continue). Pair with --gdb-halt to mirror QEMU's `-s -S`.\n\n      --gdb-halt\n          Halt the CPU at the reset vector until a GDB client connects (QEMU -S)\n\n      --no-panic-intercept\n          With --elf, don't stop at the first firmware panic: let the firmware's\n          own panic handler run, so it reboots or halts per its sdkconfig.\n          \n          For firmware that panics on purpose (a task-watchdog reset test, a\n          deliberate abort()). Symbolicated backtraces and BLE interception\n          stay.\n\n      --trace <FILE>\n          Write an execution trace to FILE in Chrome Trace Event format. Open it\n          in https://ui.perfetto.dev (or chrome://tracing) for a per-task\n          timeline, or serve the summary tables with `python3\n          tools/trace-view.py FILE`. Needs --elf: the FreeRTOS `pxCurrentTCBs`\n          symbol is what makes context switches visible\n\n      --trace-pc-period <N>\n          Sample the PC every N trace ticks (1 tick = 256 cycles) to build the\n          hot-function table. 0 disables PC sampling\n          \n          [default: 16]\n\n      --strap-mode <HEX>\n          GPIO_STRAP register value at reset (hex 0x.. or decimal)\n          \n          Mirrors QEMU's `-global\n          driver=esp32c3.gpio,property=strap_mode,value=`. 0x02 (UART0 download)\n          or 0x04 (USB-Serial/JTAG download, via joint download) resets to ROM\n          entry instead of firmware entry \u2014 pair with --uart-tcp so\n          esptool/espefuse can drive it via `socket://`. For 0x04, run esptool\n          with ESPTOOL_USB_VID_PID=303a:1001 so it detects the USB-JTAG\n          transport.\n\n      --psram-size <SIZE>\n          External RAM the board is modelled with: 4M/8M/16M/32M/64M, or 0 for\n          none\n          \n          Only the chips with external RAM take it (C5, P4, S3, S31). Firmware\n          cannot choose: `esp_psram` has no size option for P4/S31 and reads the\n          size out of the device's mode registers, so a 32 MB board is only 32\n          MB here if the emulated die says so. `0` reports no die at all, which\n          is the `CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND` path. C5's quad device names\n          2M-32M instead of 4M-64M. S3 takes either family\n          (`CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD` or `_OCT`) so it names 2M-64M; a size only\n          one family can express fits that die and reports the other absent.\n\n      --console <CONSOLE>\n          Console that stdin/stdout (and --exit-on/--inject-on) follow\n          \n          auto: UART0 until the firmware selects USB-Serial/JTAG in the ROM\n          console selector (CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG builds), then\n          EP1. A firmware with no ROM UART printer (CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_NONE with\n          the USB-Serial/JTAG secondary) shows EP1 beside UART0, with input on\n          UART0. IDF's default \"UART0 + USB-Serial/JTAG secondary\" prints once.\n          uart0: always UART0, EP1 output discarded. usb-jtag: EP1 from the\n          first byte, UART0 output discarded.\n          \n          [possible values: auto, uart0, usb-jtag]\n\n      --i2c-dev <PORT:ADDR[,OPTS]>\n          I2C device to answer on a port:\n          PORT:ADDR[,width=1|2][,fill=0xNN][,reg=REG=VAL]...\n          \n          Puts a register-file device at 7-bit address ADDR on HP I2C port PORT\n          (IDF `i2c_port_t`), beside the built-in EEPROM at 0x50. It ACKs,\n          stores what is written and reads it back \u2014 enough for an IO expander,\n          touch controller, PMIC or sensor whose driver only needs the\n          transaction to succeed. `width` is the register-address size in bytes\n          (default 1; 2 for 16-bit-register devices such as CST9xxx touch),\n          `fill` the value every register starts at (default 0xFF), and\n          `reg=REG=VAL` preloads one register, e.g. a chip-ID the driver checks.\n          Repeat the flag for each device: `--i2c-dev 0:0x20 --i2c-dev\n          0:0x5a,width=2,fill=0 --i2c-dev 0:0x34,reg=0x03=0x4a`.\n\n      --rmt-loopback <TX:RX>\n          RMT loopback pairs: TX_CH:RX_CH[,TX_CH:RX_CH...] (absolute channels)\n          \n          Feeds a TX channel's transmitted symbols into an RX channel's input\n          stream \u2014 the data equivalent of wiring the two pins together. Channel\n          numbers are the TRM's absolute indices: RX channels start at 2 on\n          C3/C6/H2 and at 4 on P4/S31 (e.g. `--rmt-loopback 0:2` loops TX ch0 to\n          RX ch2 on a C3). Lets e.g. the IDF ir_nec_transceiver example receive\n          its own transmission. The looped waveform is the pre-carrier base\n          signal, matching what a demodulating IR receiver outputs.\n\n      --log-color <WHEN>\n          Colorize firmware UART logs (default: auto; auto/always/never)\n          \n          Wraps lines starting with `I (..)`, `W (..)`, `E (..)` in IDF's\n          esp_log palette as they pass through to stdout. `auto` enables it only\n          when stdout is a TTY and `NO_COLOR` is unset. Bridged --uart-tcp\n          output is never colorized.\n\nSubcommands:\n  esp-emu update      Self-update from GitHub Releases (try `update --help`)\n  esp-emu --version   Print version and exit\n",
